description:
## Outcome

Establish the measurement authority required to distinguish real diarization progress from overfitting, helper-only success, or threshold anecdotes.

## Current evidence and limits

The existing safe-Rust diarizer has strong unit/invariant coverage and is very fast on a private local bootstrap run, but the present private reference authority is too narrow for production accuracy claims. Aggregate bootstrap results also revealed severe change-detection failure and hinted held-out regression. These measurements are diagnostic, not promotion authority.

No confidential audio, transcript text, filenames, speaker identities, timestamp excerpts, or derived identifying content may enter the repository, Git history, Beads, logs, fixtures, screenshots, or shared artifacts. Private inputs must be read in place and evaluation outputs must be aggregate and non-identifying.

## Measurement contract

Version and fingerprint:

- scorer code and metric definitions;
- corpus/split manifest for public data, or opaque local fingerprint for private evaluation;
- audio normalization and channel policy;
- reference annotation authority and overlap/collar rules;
- feature/model/configuration/calibration versions;
- toolchain and hardware for performance;
- exact commit/snapshot under test.

Metrics:

- DER and JER with declared collar/overlap policy;
- speech miss, false alarm, and speaker confusion;
- change precision/recall/F1 at multiple collars and boundary-error quantiles;
- conditional speaker-attribution accuracy and count error;
- overlap precision/recall/F1 and overlap-aware DER;
- hint adherence plus out-of-hint generalization;
- unknown precision/recall, coverage/selective risk, Brier score, ECE, and reliability bins;
- RTF, peak RSS, stage timings, bounded-state high-water marks, and determinism hash.

## Work

1. Freeze and version the scorer before model tuning.
2. Add licensed public-corpus adapters and split manifests without redistributing restricted data.
3. Maintain a local-only private evaluator that writes no source content into the project and emits only aggregate metrics plus opaque fingerprints.
4. Add a stage-aware differential runner that can compare external tools as non-authoritative oracles without making them runtime dependencies.
5. Build public-safe adversarial/metamorphic perturbations: noise, reverberation, gain, EQ/muffling, band limiting, codec/resampling, clipping, speed, channel changes, interruptions, overlap, short turns, and similar-pitch speakers.
6. Freeze v1 baselines and define non-regression/equivalence margins before inspecting held-out results.
7. Retain failures as minimized public-safe seeds and report bootstrap, development, held-out, and certification results separately.

## Success criteria

- A clean checkout can reproduce public evaluation from a repository-safe manifest and licensed/downloaded inputs.
- Private evaluation can be reproduced locally without copying or revealing source material.
- Every accuracy claim names scorer version, split fingerprint, configuration, and limitations.
- Stage attribution can distinguish normalization, timestamps, segmentation, representation, enrollment, clustering, smoothing, projection, and persistence failure.
- Promotion requires declared held-out gates; synthetic-only or single-recording private evidence remains NO-DATA for production authority.
- Accuracy evidence is separated from code hygiene and performance certification.

## Fallback

If public reference data or license authority is unavailable, run synthetic/metamorphic and local aggregate diagnostics but keep accuracy promotion blocked and explicitly report the missing authority.

PHASE 1 FOUNDATION -- No external dependencies. This bead defines the three core domain types that every other SCS bead builds on.

FILE: src/speculation.rs (new module)

WHY THESE TYPES EXIST:
The speculative streaming pipeline needs precise language for three concepts that don't exist in the codebase today:
1. A window of audio being speculatively transcribed (SpeculationWindow)
2. A tentative transcription result that may be retracted (PartialTranscript)
3. A formal correction event pairing a retraction with its replacement (CorrectionEvent)

These are NOT just TranscriptionSegment with extra fields. They carry sequence numbers for formal retraction semantics, window metadata for adaptive control, and correction provenance for the evidence ledger.

TYPES TO IMPLEMENT:

/// Represents a chunk of audio being speculatively processed.
/// The window_id is globally unique per pipeline run.
/// overlap_ms controls how much of the previous window is re-processed
/// to avoid cutting words at boundaries.
pub struct SpeculationWindow {
    pub window_id: u64,           // Monotonically increasing
    pub run_id: String,           // Links to RunReport.run_id
    pub start_ms: u64,            // Absolute position in audio stream
    pub end_ms: u64,              // start_ms + window_size_ms
    pub overlap_ms: u64,          // Overlap with previous window (typically 200-500ms)
    pub audio_hash: String,       // SHA-256 of the raw audio bytes in this window (for reproducibility)
}

/// A tentative transcription result from the fast model.
/// The seq field is globally unique and used for retraction.
/// Once emitted, a PartialTranscript is immutable -- corrections
/// create a new CorrectionEvent referencing this seq.
pub struct PartialTranscript {
    pub seq: u64,                 // Global sequence number for retraction targeting
    pub window_id: u64,           // Links to SpeculationWindow
    pub model_id: String,         // Which model produced this (e.g. "whisper-tiny")
    pub segments: Vec<TranscriptionSegment>,  // The actual transcription
    pub latency_ms: u64,          // How long the fast model took
    pub confidence_mean: f64,     // Average confidence across segments
    pub emitted_at_rfc3339: String,
    pub status: PartialStatus,    // Pending, Confirmed, Retracted
}

pub enum PartialStatus {
    Pending,    // Emitted, waiting for quality model
    Confirmed,  // Quality model agreed (within tolerance)
    Retracted,  // Quality model disagreed, correction emitted
}

/// A formal correction event. This is the atomic unit of cancel-correct:
/// it pairs a retraction (seq of the original partial) with the corrected
/// transcription from the quality model.
pub struct CorrectionEvent {
    pub correction_id: u64,       // Unique ID for this correction
    pub retracted_seq: u64,       // The PartialTranscript.seq being retracted
    pub window_id: u64,           // Links to SpeculationWindow
    pub quality_model_id: String, // Which model produced the correction (e.g. "whisper-large")
    pub corrected_segments: Vec<TranscriptionSegment>,
    pub quality_latency_ms: u64,  // How long the quality model took
    pub quality_confidence_mean: f64,
    pub drift: CorrectionDrift,   // Metrics on how different the correction was
    pub corrected_at_rfc3339: String,
}

/// Quantifies how much the quality model's output differs from the fast model's.
/// Used by the adaptive controller to tune window size.
pub struct CorrectionDrift {
    pub wer_approx: f64,          // Word Error Rate approximation (0.0 = identical, 1.0 = completely different)
    pub confidence_delta: f64,    // Absolute mean confidence difference
    pub segment_count_delta: i32, // Difference in number of segments (quality - fast)
    pub text_edit_distance: usize,// Levenshtein distance on concatenated text
}

ALSO ADD:
- SpeculationWindow::new(), duration_ms(), contains_ms()
- PartialTranscript::new(), confirm(), retract()
- CorrectionEvent::new(), is_significant() (drift above threshold)
- CorrectionDrift::compute(fast_segments, quality_segments) -- static constructor
- Serde derives on all types (Serialize, Deserialize, Debug, Clone)
- Register `mod speculation;` in lib.rs
- Make all types pub for integration test access

TESTING EXPECTATIONS (separate bead scs.12):
- Round-trip serde for all types
- CorrectionDrift::compute produces expected values for known inputs
- PartialStatus transitions are valid
- SpeculationWindow overlap calculations are correct

PHASE 4 SURFACE INTEGRATION -- Wires the speculative streaming pipeline into the CLI and existing orchestrator.

FILES: src/cli.rs (extend), src/orchestrator.rs (extend)

WHY:
The SpeculativeStreamingPipeline (scs.6) needs to be invocable from the CLI. This bead adds the --speculative flag (with sub-options) and wires it into the existing pipeline so it replaces the Backend stage when active.

CLI ADDITIONS:

    /// Enable speculative cancel-correct streaming mode.
    /// Runs a fast model for instant results while a quality model
    /// confirms or corrects in parallel.
    #[arg(long)]
    pub speculative: bool,

    /// Fast model for speculative mode (default: auto-select smallest available)
    #[arg(long, requires = "speculative")]
    pub fast_model: Option<String>,

    /// Quality model for speculative mode (default: auto-select largest available)
    #[arg(long, requires = "speculative")]
    pub quality_model: Option<String>,

    /// Initial speculation window size in milliseconds (default: 3000)
    #[arg(long, requires = "speculative", default_value = "3000")]
    pub window_size_ms: u64,

    /// Window overlap in milliseconds (default: 500)
    #[arg(long, requires = "speculative", default_value = "500")]
    pub overlap_ms: u64,

    /// Maximum WER tolerance before correction (default: 0.1)
    #[arg(long, requires = "speculative", default_value = "0.1")]
    pub correction_tolerance_wer: f64,

    /// Disable adaptive window sizing
    #[arg(long, requires = "speculative")]
    pub no_adaptive: bool,

    /// Force all windows to use quality model result (evaluation mode)
    #[arg(long, requires = "speculative")]
    pub always_correct: bool,

ORCHESTRATOR INTEGRATION:
In orchestrator.rs, the run_pipeline() function currently runs the Backend stage like:
  let result = engine.run(request, normalized_wav, work_dir, timeout)?;

With --speculative, this becomes:
  if config.speculative {
      let pipeline = SpeculativeStreamingPipeline::new(spec_config, fast_engine, quality_engine, pcx);
      let result = pipeline.process_file(normalized_wav, request)?;
      // The pipeline handles event emission internally
  } else {
      let result = engine.run(request, normalized_wav, work_dir, timeout)?;
  }

ENGINE SELECTION:
- If --fast-model and --quality-model are specified: use those
- If only --speculative: auto-select based on available engines
  - Fast: prefer whisper-tiny > whisper-small > whisper-base
  - Quality: prefer whisper-large > whisper-medium > whisper-small
  - If only one engine is available: error ("speculative mode requires at least two distinct models")
- The auto-selection uses the existing all_engines() discovery

ROBOT MODE INTERACTION:
- In robot mode (--robot): emit all speculation events to stdout as NDJSON
- In TUI mode (--tui): drive LiveTranscriptionView with correction updates
- In plain mode: show corrections inline with [CORRECTED] markers

RUN REPORT:
The RunReport should include:
  - speculation_stats: SpeculationStats (in the evidence array)
  - All correction events in the events array
  - CorrectionEvidenceLedger in the evidence array

DEPENDS ON: scs.6 (pipeline), scs.7 (adaptive controller)
